helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3147569				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_EDIT_TP_DROPCAPS_EDT_TEXT\"\>Enter the text that you want to display as drop caps instead of the first letters of the paragraph.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150763				0	ga	Character Style				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3151181				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_LISTBOX_TP_DROPCAPS_BOX_TEMPLATE\"\>Select the formatting style that you want to apply to the drop caps.\</ahelp\> To use the formatting style of the current paragraph, select [None].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\selection_mode.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ga	Selection Mode				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\selection_mode.xhp	0	help	hd_id4177678				0	ga	\<link href=\"text/swriter/01/selection_mode.xhp\"\>Selection Mode\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\selection_mode.xhp	0	help	par_id2962126				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Choose the selection mode from the submenu: normal selection mode, or block selection mode.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\selection_mode.xhp	0	help	par_id9816278				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>In normal selection mode, you can select multi-line text including the line ends.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\selection_mode.xhp	0	help	par_id3097323				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>In block selection mode, you can select a rectangular block of text.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ga	Defining Conditions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145828				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>logical expressions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulating conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>conditions; in fields and sections\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>fields;defining conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>sections;defining conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>variables; in conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>user data;in conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>databases;in conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>hiding; database fields\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145828				0	ga	\<variable id=\"defining_conditions\"\>\<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04090200.xhp\"\>Defining Conditions\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145242				0	ga	Conditions are logical expressions that you can use to control the display of \<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04090000.xhp\"\>fields\</link\> and \<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04020000.xhp\"\>sections\</link\> in your document. Although the following examples apply to fields, they also apply to sections.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147171				0	ga	You can define conditions for the following field types:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151185				0	ga	Conditional text: displays text A if the condition is true, or text B if the condition is false.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149289				0	ga	Hidden text: hides the contents of the field if the condition is true.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145412				0	ga	Hidden paragraph: hides the paragraph if the condition is true.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147515				0	ga	Any record and next record: controls the access to database records.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149802	18			0	ga	The simplest way to define a condition is to type the logical expression directly in a \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box using the following values:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153677				0	ga	TRUE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152960				0	ga	The condition is always met. You can also enter any value not equal to 0 as the conditional text.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155900				0	ga	FALSE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154191				0	ga	The condition is not met. You can also enter the value 0.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147090	23			0	ga	If you leave the \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box empty, the condition is interpreted as not being met.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148980				0	ga	When you define a condition, use the same \<link href=\"text/swriter/02/14020000.xhp\"\>elements\</link\> for defining a formula, namely comparative operators, mathematical and statistical functions, number formats, variables and constants.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153638				0	ga	You can use the following types of variables when you define a condition:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155135				0	ga	Predefined \<link href=\"text/swriter/02/14020000.xhp\"\>$[officename] variables\</link\> that use statistics on document properties				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156273				0	ga	Custom variables, that are a created with the "Set variable" field				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149174				0	ga	Variables based on user data				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145781				0	ga	Variables based on the contents of database fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155916				0	ga	You cannot use internal variables, such as page and chapter numbers, in condition expression.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151375				0	ga	Conditions and Variables				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150122				0	ga	The following examples use a variable called "x":				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147417				0	ga	x == 1 or x EQ 1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147500				0	ga	The condition is true if "x" is equal to 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145259				0	ga	x != 1 or x NEQ 1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150551				0	ga	The condition is true if "x" does not equal 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147749				0	ga	sinx == 0				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146345				0	ga	The condition is true if "x" is a multiple of pi.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149846				0	ga	To use comparative operators with strings, the operands must be bounded by double quotation marks:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151078				0	ga	x == "ABC" or x EQ "ABC"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150162				0	ga	Checks if variable "x" contains (true) the "ABC" string, or not (false).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153301				0	ga	x == "" or x EQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156120				0	ga	nó				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156133				0	ga	!x or NOT x				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156112				0	ga	Checks if the variable "x" contains an empty string.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150097				0	ga	The "equal" comparative operator must be represented by two equal signs (==) in a condition. For example, if you define a variable "x" with the value of 1, you can enter the condition as x==1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148791				0	ga	User Data				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150028				0	ga	You can include user data when you define conditions. To change your user data, choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - $[officename] - User data\</emph\>. User data must be entered in the form of strings. You can query the user data with "==" (EQ), "!=" (NEQ), or "!"(NOT).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153124				0	ga	The following table lists user data variables and their meanings:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149632				0	ga	Luaineach				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150662				0	ga	Meaning				2002-02-02 02:02:02
