helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149578				0	ga	5.75 as 5 3/4 and 6.3 as 6 3/10				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156152				0	ga	.5 as 0.5				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149762				0	ga	0.##				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149276				0	ga	Thousands Separator				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154380				0	ga	Depending on your language setting, you can use a comma or a period as a thousands separator. You can also use the separator to reduce the size of the number that is displayed by a multiple of 1000.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154905				0	ga	Number Format				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150822				0	ga	Format Code				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147264				0	ga	15000 as 15,000				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154935				0	ga	16000 as 16				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154836				0	ga	Including Text in Number Format Codes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150398				0	ga	Text and Numbers				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154224				0	ga	To include text in a number format that is applied to a cell containing numbers, place a double quotation mark (") in front of and behind the text, or a backslash (\\) before a single character. For example, enter \<emph\>#.# "meters"\</emph\> to display "3.5 meters" or \<emph\>#.# \\m\</emph\> to display "3.5 m".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148979				0	ga	Text and Text				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153338				0	ga	To include text in a number format that is applied to a cell that might contain text, enclose the text by double quotation marks (" "), and then add an at sign (@). For example, enter \<emph\>"Total for "@\</emph\> to display "Total for December".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154330				0	ga	Spaces				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156294				0	ga	To use a character to define the width of a space in a number format, type an underscore ( _ ) followed by the character. The width of the space varies according to the width of the character that you choose. For example, \<emph\>_M\</emph\> creates a wider space than \<emph\>_i\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155994				0	ga	Color				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156423				0	ga	To set the color of a section of a number format code, insert one of the following color names in square brackets [ ]:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154630				0	ga	CYAN				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148676				0	ga	GREEN				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154123				0	ga	BLACK				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149167				0	ga	BLUE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3158407				0	ga	MAGENTA				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149560				0	ga	RED				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147502				0	ga	WHITE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	ga	YELLOW				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147435				0	ga	Conditions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148575				0	ga	Conditional Brackets				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3155312				0	ga	You can define a number format so that it only applies when the condition that you specify is met. Conditions are enclosed by square brackets [ ].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159179				0	ga	You can use any combination of numbers and the <, <=, >, >=, = and <> operators.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159196				0	ga	For example, if you want to apply different colors to different temperature data, enter:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150872				0	ga	[BLUE][<0]#,0 "°C";[RED][>30]#,0 "°C";[BLACK]#,0 "°C"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3157870				0	ga	All temperatures below zero are blue, temperatures between 0 and 30 °C are black, and temperatures higher than 30 °C are red.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154833				0	ga	Positive and Negative Numbers				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147295				0	ga	To define a number format that adds a different text to a number depending on if the number is positive, negative, or equal to zero, use the following format:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153727				0	ga	"plus" 0;"minus" 0;"null" 0				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149260				0	ga	Percentages and Scientific Notation				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147218				0	ga	Percentages				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151168				0	ga	To display numbers as percentages, add the percent sign (%) to the number format.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156005				0	ga	Scientific Notation				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3146923				0	ga	Scientific notation lets you write very large numbers or very small fractions in a compact form. For example, in scientific notation, 650000 is written as 6.5 x 10^5, and 0.000065 as 6.5 x 10^-5. In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\>, these numbers are written as 6.5E+5 and 6.5E-5, respectively. To create a number format that displays numbers using scientific notation, enter a # or 0, and then one of the following codes E-, E+, e- or e+.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159080				0	ga	Number Format Codes of Currency Formats				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147318				0	ga	The default currency format for the cells in your spreadsheet is determined by the regional setting of your operating system. If you want, you can apply a custom currency symbol to a cell. For example, enter #,##0.00 € to display 4.50 € (Euros).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150032				0	ga	You can also specify the locale setting for the currency by entering the locale code for the country after the symbol. For example, [$€-407] represents Euros in Germany. To view the locale code for a country, select the country in the \<emph\>Language\</emph\> list on the \<emph\>Numbers\</emph\> tab of the \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3157309				0	ga	Date and Time Formats				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153740				0	ga	Date Formats				2002-02-02 02:02:02
