helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\copytext2application.xhp	0	help	par_id3156426				0	ga	You can insert text into other document types, such as spreadsheets and presentations. Note that there is a difference between whether the text is inserted into a text frame, a spreadsheet cell, or into the outline view of a presentation.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\copytext2application.xhp	0	help	par_id3147576				0	ga	If you copy text to the clipboard, you can paste it with or without text attributes. Use the shortcut keys \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+C to copy and \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+V to paste.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\copytext2application.xhp	0	help	par_id3152349				0	ga	\<image id=\"img_id3143270\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_paste.png\" width=\"5.64mm\" height=\"5.64mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3143270\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\copytext2application.xhp	0	help	par_id3158430				0	ga	To select the format in which the clipboard contents will be pasted, click the arrow next to the \<emph\>Paste\</emph\> icon on the Standard bar, or choose \<emph\>Edit - Paste Special\</emph\>, then select the proper format.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\copytext2application.xhp	0	help	par_id3156155				0	ga	If a text document contains headings formatted with the Heading Paragraph Style, choose \<emph\>File - Send - Outline to Presentation\</emph\>. A new presentation document is created, which contains the headings as an outline.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\copytext2application.xhp	0	help	par_id3145316				0	ga	If you want to transfer each heading together with its accompanying paragraphs, select the \<emph\>File - Send - AutoAbstract to Presentation\</emph\> command. You must have formatted the headings with a corresponding Paragraph Style to be able to see this command.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\copytext2application.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156024				0	ga	Copying Text Using Drag-and-Drop				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\copytext2application.xhp	0	help	par_id3147303				0	ga	If you select text and drag it into a spreadsheet with drag-and-drop, it will be inserted as text into the cell where you release the mouse.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\copytext2application.xhp	0	help	par_id3149655				0	ga	If you drag text to the normal view of a presentation, an OLE object is inserted as a $[officename] plug-in.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\copytext2application.xhp	0	help	par_id3150793				0	ga	If you drag the text to the outline view of a presentation, it will be inserted at the cursor location.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ga	Searching With a Form Filter				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	bm_id8772545				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>form filters\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>databases;form filters\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>searching; form filters\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>removing;form filters\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>filtering; data in forms\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data;filtering in forms\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>forms; filtering data\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data, see also values\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156042				0	ga	\<variable id=\"data_search2\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/data_search2.xhp\" name=\"Searching With a Form Filter\"\>Searching With a Form Filter\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	par_id3149182				0	ga	Open a form document that contains database fields.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	par_id3159157				0	ga	As an example, open an empty text document and press F4. Open the bibliography database table \<emph\>biblio\</emph\> in the data source view. While pressing Shift+\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>, drag a few column headers into the document so that the form fields are created.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	par_id3150984	19			0	ga	On the \<emph\>Form Controls\</emph\> toolbar, click the \<emph\>Design Mode On/Off\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3147618\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_switchcontroldesignmode.png\" width=\"0.222inch\" height=\"0.222inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3147618\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\> to turn off the design mode.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	par_id3148672	6			0	ga	On the \<emph\>Form Navigation\</emph\> toolbar, click the \<emph\>Form-Based Filters\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3149807\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_formfilter.png\" width=\"0.222inch\" height=\"0.222inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3149807\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>. The current document is displayed with its form controls as an empty edit mask. The \<emph\>Form Filter \</emph\>toolbar appears.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	par_id3149666				0	ga	Enter the filter conditions into one or several fields. Note that if you enter filter conditions into several fields, all of the entered conditions must match (Boolean AND).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	par_id3149481	8			0	ga	More information about wildcards and operators can be found in \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"Query Design\"\>Query Design\</link\>. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	par_id3152462				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".uno:FormFilterExecute\"\>If you click the \<emph\>Apply Form-Based Filter\</emph\> icon on the \<emph\>Form Filter\</emph\> toolbar, the filter will be applied.\</ahelp\> You see the \<emph\>Form Navigation\</emph\> toolbar and can browse through the found records.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	par_id3145273				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".uno:FormFilterExit\"\>If you click on the \<emph\>Close\</emph\> button on the \<emph\>Form Filter\</emph\> toolbar, the form is displayed without a filter.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	par_id3150114	11			0	ga	Click the \<link href=\"text/shared/02/12120000.xhp\" name=\"Apply Filter\"\>\<emph\>Apply Filter\</emph\>\</link\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3144764\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_datafilterstandardfilter.png\" width=\"0.222inch\" height=\"0.222inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3144764\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\> on the \<emph\>Form Navigation\</emph\> toolbar to change to the filtered view.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_search2.xhp	0	help	par_id3146898	12			0	ga	The filter that has been set can be removed by clicking \<link href=\"text/shared/02/12040000.xhp\" name=\"Remove Filter/Sort\"\>\<emph\>Remove Filter/Sort\</emph\>\</link\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3151318\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_removefiltersort.png\" width=\"0.222inch\" height=\"0.222inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3151318\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ga	About Digital Signatures				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	bm_id7430951				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>certificates\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>digital signatures;overview\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>security;digital signatures\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id2767418				0	ga	\<variable id=\"digital_signatures\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/digital_signatures.xhp\"\>About Digital Signatures\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10632				0	ga	In %PRODUCTNAME, you can digitally sign your documents and macros. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id6564531				0	ga	Certificates				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10639				0	ga	To sign a document digitally, you need a personal key, the certificate. A personal key is stored on your computer as a combination of a private key, which must be kept secret, and a public key, which you add to your documents when you sign them.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066D				0	ga	Save and sign the document				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10671				0	ga	When you apply a digital signature to a document, a kind of checksum is computed from the document's content plus your personal key. The checksum and your public key are stored together with the document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10674				0	ga	Open a signed document				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10678				0	ga	When someone later opens the document on any computer with a recent version of %PRODUCTNAME, the program will compute the checksum again and compare it with the stored checksum. If both are the same, the program will signal that you see the original, unchanged document. In addition, the program can show you the public key information from the certificate.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN1067B				0	ga	You can compare the public key with the public key that is published on the web site of the certificate authority.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN1067E				0	ga	Whenever someone changes something in the document, this change breaks the digital signature. After the change, there will be no sign that you see the original document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id2008200911381426				0	ga	The result of the signature validation is displayed in the status bar and within the Digital Signature dialog. Several documents and macro signatures can exist inside an ODF document. If there is a problem with one signature, then the validation result of that one signature is assumed for all signatures. That is, if there are ten valid signatures and one invalid signature, then the status bar and the status field in the dialog will flag the signature as invalid.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200911571878				0	ga	You can see any of the following icons and messages when you open a signed document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504050				0	ga	Icon in Status bar				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504061				0	ga	Signature status				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504010				0	ga	\<image id=\"img_id0821200912421569\" src=\"xmlsecurity/res/certificate_16.png\" width=\"0.1665in\" height=\"0.1665in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id0821200912421569\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504189				0	ga	The signature is valid.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id082120091250418				0	ga	\<image id=\"img_id0821200912431081\" src=\"xmlsecurity/res/notcertificate_16.png\" width=\"0.1665in\" height=\"0.1665in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id0821200912431081\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504133				0	ga	The signature is OK, but the certificates could not be validated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504165				0	ga	The signature and the certificate are OK, but not all parts of the document are signed. (For documents that were signed with old versions of the software, see note below.)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504237				0	ga	\<image id=\"img_id0821200912435090\" src=\"xmlsecurity/res/caution_11x16.png\" width=\"0.1665in\" height=\"0.1146in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id0821200912435090\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504233				0	ga	The signature is invalid.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id0821200910191787				0	ga	Signatures and software versions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200910191747				0	ga	The signing of contents got changed with OpenOffice.org 3.2 and StarOffice 9.2. Now all contents of the files, except the signature file itself (META-INF/documentsignatures.xml) are signed. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200910191774				0	ga	When you sign a document with OpenOffice.org 3.2 or StarOffice 9.2 or a later version, and you open that document in an older version of the software, the signature will be displayed as "invalid". Signatures created with older versions of the software will be marked with "only parts of the documents are signed" when loaded in the newer software.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id2008200911583098				0	ga	When you load an ODF document, you might see an icon in the status bar and the status field in the dialog that indicates that the document is only partially signed. This status will appear when the signature and certificate are valid, but they were created with a version of OpenOffice.org before 3.2 or StarOffice before 9.2. In versions of OpenOffice.org before 3.0 or StarOffice before 9.0, the document signature was applied to the main contents, pictures and embedded objects only and some contents, like macros, were not signed. In OpenOffice.org 3.0 and StarOffice 9.0 the document signature was applied to most content, including macros. However, the mimetype and the content of the META-INF folder were not signed. And in OpenOffice.org 3.2 and StarOffice 9.2 all contents, except the signature file itself (META-INF/documentsignatures.xml), are signed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id9354228				0	ga	Security Warnings				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id2372508				0	ga	When you receive a signed document, and the software reports that the signature is valid, this does not mean that you can be absolutely sure that the document is the same that the sender has sent. Signing documents with software certificates is not a perfectly secure method. Numerous ways are possible to circumvent the security features.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id7953123				0	ga	Example: Think about someone who wants to camouflage his identity to be a sender from your bank. He can easily get a certificate using a false name, then send you any signed e-mail pretending he is working for your bank. You will get that e-mail, and the e-mail or the document within has the "valid signed" icon. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id6195257				0	ga	Do not trust the icon. Inspect and verify the certificates.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id8635517				0	ga	The validation of a signature is not a legally binding guarantee of any kind.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id6075624				0	ga	On Windows operating systems, the Windows features of validating a signature are used. On Solaris and Linux systems, files that are supplied by Thunderbird, Mozilla or Firefox are used. You must ensure that the files that are in use within your system are really the original files that were supplied by the original developers. For malevolent intruders, there are numerous ways to replace original files with other files that they supply.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id6819971				0	ga	The messages about validation of a signature that you see in %PRODUCTNAME are the messages that the validation files return. The %PRODUCTNAME software has no way to ensure that the messages reflect the true status of any certificate. The %PRODUCTNAME software only displays the messages that other files that are not under control of %PRODUCTNAME report. There is no legal responsibility of %PRODUCTNAME that the displayed messages reflect the true status of a digital signature.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id3204443				0	ga	\<link href=\"http://wiki.services.openoffice.org/wiki/How_to_use_digital_Signatures\"\>English Wiki page on digital signatures\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
