helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151220	260			0	ga	$[officename] also supports the following \<emph\>Escape sequences\</emph\> known from ODBC and JDBC:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157975	261			0	ga	Date				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149753	262			0	ga	{d 'YYYY-MM-DD'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156318	263			0	ga	Time				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151280	264			0	ga	{t 'HH:MI:SS[.SS]'} - [ ] optional				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153264	265			0	ga	Dáta/Am				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153981	266			0	ga	{ts 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MI:SS[.SS]'} - [ ] optional				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149539				0	ga	Example: select {d '1999-12-31'} from world.years				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146073	268			0	ga	\<emph\>Like \</emph\>Escape Sequence: {escape 'escape-character'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150661				0	ga	Example: select * from Item where ItemName like 'The *%' {escape '*'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148541				0	ga	The example will give you all of the entries where the item name begins with 'The *'. This means that you can also search for characters that would otherwise be interpreted as placeholders, such as *, ?, _, % or the period.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150572				0	ga	\<emph\>Outer Join\</emph\> Escape Sequence: {oj outer-join}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156052				0	ga	Example: select Article.* from {oj item LEFT OUTER JOIN orders ON item.no=orders.ANR}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153674				0	ga	Querying text fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149134				0	ga	To query the content of a text field, you must put the expression between single quotes. The distinction between uppercase and lowercase letters depends on the database in use. LIKE, by definition, is case-sensitive (though some databases don't see it that strict).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149302				0	ga	Querying date fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150510				0	ga	Even if you want to filter by a date, you must place the expression between single quotation marks. The following formats are valid: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS and YYYY/MM/DD HH:MM:SS as well as YYYY.MM.DD HH:MM:SS				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150427				0	ga	Querying Yes/No fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149523				0	ga	To query Yes/No fields, use the following syntax for dBASE tables:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153180				0	ga	Status				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147481				0	ga	Query criterion				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155187				0	ga	Sampla				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156092				0	ga	Tá				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152414				0	ga	for dBASE tables: not equal to any given value				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151265				0	ga	=1 returns all records where the Yes/No field has the status "Yes" or "On" (selected in black),				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152450				0	ga	Níl				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155331				0	ga	=0 returns all records for which the Yes/No field has the status "No" or "Off" (no selection).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154179				0	ga	Null				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147035				0	ga	IS NULL				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159385				0	ga	IS NULL returns all records for which the Yes/No field has neither of the states Yes or No (selected in gray).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157888				0	ga	The syntax depends on the database system used. You should also note that Yes/No fields can be defined differently (only 2 states instead of 3).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145772				0	ga	Parameter queries				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157312				0	ga	You must place the variable between square brackets (=[x]) to create a query with variable parameters. Alternatively, you can use an equal sign followed by a colon (=:x). When the query is executed, the program will display a dialog asking you for the expression to which the variable x should be assigned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150818				0	ga	If you query several parameters at the same time, you will see a list field in the dialog containing all of the parameters and an input line alongside each one. Enter the values, preferably from top to bottom, and press the Enter key after each line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157855				0	ga	Parameter queries with placeholders (*, _) or special characters (for example, ?) are not possible.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157537				0	ga	If you formulate a parameter query and you save it with the variables, you can later create a query in which only the variables have to be replaced by the expressions that you want. $[officename] asks for these variables in a dialog as soon as you open the query.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151035				0	ga	Ionchur Paraiméadar				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153596				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>The \<emph\>Parameter Input\</emph\> dialog asks you which variables you defined in the query. Enter a value for each query variable and confirm by clicking \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150585				0	ga	Parameter queries are also used for \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170203.xhp\" name=\"subforms\"\>subforms\</link\>, since they work exclusively with queries for which the values to be invoked are read internally from a variable.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153645				0	ga	A parameter query can have the following form in an SQL statement:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156731				0	ga	select * from 'addresses' where 'name' = :placeholder				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145181				0	ga	SQL Mode				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147013				0	ga	SQL stands for "Structured Query Language" and describes instructions for updating and administering relational databases.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152570	136			0	ga	In $[officename] you do not need any knowledge of SQL for most queries, since you do not have to enter the SQL code. If you create a query in the query design, $[officename] automatically converts your instructions into the corresponding SQL syntax. If, with the help of the \<emph\>Switch Design View On/Off \</emph\>button, you change to the SQL view, you can see the SQL commands for a query that has been created previously.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152412				0	ga	You can formulate your query directly in the SQL code. Note, however, that the special syntax is dependent upon the database system that you use.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146842				0	ga	If you enter the SQL code manually, you can create SQL-specific queries that are not supported by the graphical interface in \<emph\>Query design\</emph\>. These queries must be executed in native SQL mode.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149632				0	ga	By clicking the \<link href=\"text/shared/02/14030000.xhp\" name=\"Run SQL command directly\"\>\<emph\>Run SQL command directly\</emph\>\</link\> icon in the SQL view, you can formulate a query that is not processed by $[officename].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ga	Properties 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	hd_id8836939				0	ga	\<variable id=\"rep_prop\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/rep_prop.xhp\"\>Properties\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id2711264				0	ga	The Properties window of the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/rep_main.xhp\"\>Report Builder\</link\> always shows the properties of the currently selected object in the Report Builder view.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id1080660				0	ga	Press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Shift-F1\</item\> and point with the mouse at an input box to see a help text for this input box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id7175817				0	ga	On first start of the Report Builder, the Properties window shows the \<emph\>Data\</emph\> tab page for the whole report.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id9895931				0	ga	Select a table from the Contents list, then press Tab or click outside the input box to leave the input box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id3587145				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>The Add Field window is shown automatically when you have selected a table in the Contents box and leave that box. You can also click the Add Field icon on the toolbar, or choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>View - Add Field\</item\>.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id6993926				0	ga	The \<emph\>General\</emph\> tab page can be used to change the name of the report, and to disable the Page Header or Page Footer areas, among others.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id3729361				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>To display the Data or General tab page for the whole report, choose Edit - Select Report.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id1768852				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Groups are kept together by page or by column (default). You must enable Keep Together also.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id6304818				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Specifies in which context the page header will be printed: on all pages, or not on pages with a report header or footer.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id401623				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Specifies in which context the page footer will be printed: on all pages, or not on pages with a report header or footer\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id2162236				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Specifies to print repeated values.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id7022003				0	ga	If you click the Page Header or Page Footer area without selecting any object, you see the \<emph\>General\</emph\> tab page for that area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id7004303				0	ga	You can edit some visual properties for the area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id2561723				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Sets the background color for the selected object, both on screen and for printing.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id1064485				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>An invisible object is not shown in the executed report. It is still visible in the Report Builder view.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id2356028				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Defines the height of the selected object.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id1404461				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>If the Conditional Print Expression evaluates to TRUE, the selected object will be printed.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id7404705				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Specifies whether the background of the selected object is transparent or opaque.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id7466963				0	ga	If you click the \<emph\>Detail\</emph\> area without selecting any object, you see the \<emph\>General\</emph\> tab page for that area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id3644215				0	ga	You can specify some properties to fine-tune the way the records are printed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id3148899				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Force New Page specifies whether the current section and/or the next section is printed on a new page.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id6164433				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>New Row Or Column specifies, for a multi-column design, whether the current section and/or the next section will be printed on a new row or column.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id7405011				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Keep Together specifies to print the current object starting on top of a new page if it doesn't fit on the current page.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id1536606				0	ga	Insert some data fields into the Detail area, or insert other control fields into any area. When you select an inserted field, you can set the properties in the Properties window.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id9631641				0	ga	For a Label field, you can change the displayed text in the Label input box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id7749565				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>For a picture, you can specify to either insert the picture as a link to a file or only as an embedded object in the Base file. The embedded option increases the size of the Base file, while the link option is not as portable to other computers.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id4041871				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Set the X Position for the selected object\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
