helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id6293527				0	ga	To return the angle in degrees, use the DEGREES function.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154054				0	ga	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3143229				0	ga	\<item type=\"input\"\>=ATAN(1)\</item\> returns 0.785398163397448 (PI/4 radians).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id8746299				0	ga	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DEGREES(ATAN(1))\</item\> returns 45. The tangent of 45 degrees is 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153983				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>ATAN2 function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153983				0	ga	ATAN2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3154297				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ARCTAN2\"\>Returns the inverse trigonometric tangent of the specified x and y coordinates.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149758				0	ga	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3156013				0	ga	ATAN2(NumberX; NumberY)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3151168				0	ga	\<emph\>NumberX\</emph\> is the value of the x coordinate.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3152798				0	ga	\<emph\>NumberY\</emph\> is the value of the y coordinate.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id5036164				0	ga	ATAN2 returns the inverse trigonometric tangent, that is, the angle (in radians) between the x-axis and a line from point NumberX, NumberY to the origin. The angle returned is between -PI and PI.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3001800				0	ga	To return the angle in degrees, use the DEGREES function.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145663				0	ga	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3154692				0	ga	\<item type=\"input\"\>=ATAN2(20;20)\</item\> returns 0.785398163397448 (PI/4 radians).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id1477095				0	ga	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DEGREES(ATAN2(12.3;12.3))\</item\> returns 45. The tangent of 45 degrees is 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155398				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>ATANH function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155398				0	ga	ATANH				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3148829				0	ga	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ARTANHYP\"\>Returns the inverse hyperbolic tangent of a number.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146997				0	ga	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3149912				0	ga	ATANH(Number)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3150521	134			0	ga	 This function returns the inverse hyperbolic tangent of \<emph\>Number\</emph\>, that is the number whose hyperbolic tangent is Number. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id9357280				0	ga	 Number must obey the condition -1 < number < 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148450				0	ga	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3145419				0	ga	\<item type=\"input\"\>=ATANH(0)\</item\> returns 0.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153062				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>COS function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
