helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153252				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>cell protection; unprotecting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>protecting; unprotecting cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>unprotecting cells\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153252	14			0	ga	\<variable id=\"cell_unprotect\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cell_unprotect.xhp\" name=\"Unprotecting Cells\"\>Unprotecting Cells\</link\> \</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	par_id3151112				0	ga	Click the sheet for which you want to cancel the protection.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	par_id3149656				0	ga	Select \<emph\>Tools - Protect Document\</emph\>, then choose \<emph\>Sheet\</emph\> or \<emph\>Document\</emph\> to remove the check mark indicating the protected status.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	par_id3145171				0	ga	If you have assigned a password, enter it in this dialog and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	par_id3153771				0	ga	The cells can now be edited, the formulas can be viewed, and all cells can be printed until you reactivate the protection for the sheet or document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ga	Filtering Cell Ranges 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153541				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>cell ranges;applying/removing filters\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>filtering;cell ranges/database ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>database ranges;applying/removing filters\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>removing;cell range filters\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153541				0	ga	\<variable id=\"database_filter\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/database_filter.xhp\" name=\"Filtering Cell Ranges\"\>Filtering Cell Ranges\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3145069				0	ga	You can use several filters to filter cell ranges in spreadsheets. A standard filter uses the options that you specify to filter the data. An AutoFilter filters data according to a specific value or string. An advanced filter uses filter criteria from specified cells.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN10682				0	ga	To Apply a Standard Filter to a Cell Range				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3150398				0	ga	Click in a cell range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN10693				0	ga	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Filter - Standard Filter\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3156422				0	ga	In the \<emph\>Standard Filter\</emph\> dialog, specify the filter options that you want.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A5				0	ga	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153143				0	ga	The records that match the filter options that you specified are shown.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153728				0	ga	To Apply an AutoFilter to a Cell Range				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3144764				0	ga	Click in a cell range or a database range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id9303872				0	ga	If you want to apply multiple AutoFilters to the same sheet, you must first define database ranges, then apply the AutoFilters to the database ranges.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3154944				0	ga	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Filter - AutoFilter\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN106DB				0	ga	An arrow button is added to the head of each column in the database range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153878				0	ga	Click the arrow button in the column that contains the value or string that you want to set as the filter criteria.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN10749				0	ga	Select the value or string that you want to use as the filter criteria.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN1074C				0	ga	The records that match the filter criteria that you selected are shown.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN106E8				0	ga	To Remove a Filter From a Cell Range				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN1075C				0	ga	Click in a filtered cell range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN106EC				0	ga	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Filter - Remove Filter\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id4525284				0	ga	\<link href=\"http://wiki.services.openoffice.org/wiki/Documentation/How_Tos/Defining_a_Data_Range\"\>Wiki page about defining a data range\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ga	Applying Sort Lists				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150870				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>filling;customized lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sort lists;applying\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defining;sort lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>geometric lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>arithmetic lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>series;sort lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>lists; user-defined\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>customized lists\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150870	3			0	ga	\<variable id=\"sorted_list\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/sorted_list.xhp\" name=\"Applying Sort Lists\"\>Applying Sort Lists\</link\> \</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id3159154				0	ga	Sort lists allow you to type one piece of information in a cell, then drag it to fill in a consecutive list of items.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id3148645				0	ga	For example, enter the text "Jan" or "January" in an empty cell. Select the cell and click the mouse on the lower right corner of the cell border. Then drag the selected cell a few cells to the right or downwards. When you release the mouse button, the highlighted cells will be filled with the names of the months.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id2367931				0	ga	Hold down \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>if you do not want to fill the cells with different values.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577				0	ga	The predefined series can be found under \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Sort Lists\</emph\>. You can also create your own lists of text strings tailored to your needs, such as a list of your company's branch offices. When you use the information in these lists later (for example, as headings), just enter the first name in the list and expand the entry by dragging it with your mouse.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id3147434				0	ga	\<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060400.xhp\" name=\"Sort lists\"\>Sort lists\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ga	Automatically Calculating Series				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>series; calculating\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>calculating; series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>linear series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>growth series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>date series\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>powers of 2 calculations\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; filling automatically\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>automatic cell filling\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>AutoFill function\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>filling;cells, automatically\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769				0	ga	\<variable id=\"calc_series\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_series.xhp\" name=\"Automatically Calculating Series\"\>Automatically Filling in Data Based on Adjacent Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A8				0	ga	You can automatically fill cells with data with the AutoFill command or the Series command.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D3				0	ga	Using AutoFill				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D7				0	ga	AutoFill automatically generates a data series based on a defined pattern.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	ga	On a sheet, click in a cell, and type a number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106CB				0	ga	Click in another cell and then click back in the cell where you typed the number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272				0	ga	Drag the fill handle in the bottom right corner of the cell across the cells that you want to fill, and release the mouse button.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3145801				0	ga	The cells are filled with ascending numbers.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106EE				0	ga	To quickly create a list of consecutive days, enter \<item type=\"literal\"\>Monday\</item\> in a cell, and drag the fill handle.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id9720145				0	ga	Hold down \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>if you do not want to fill the cells with different values.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490				0	ga	If you select two or more adjacent cells that contain different numbers, and drag, the remaining cells are filled with the arithmetic pattern that is recognized in the numbers. The AutoFill function also recognizes customized lists that are defined under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Sort Lists\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10737				0	ga	You can double-click the fill handle to automatically fill all empty columns of the current data block. For example, first enter Jan into A1 and drag the fill handle down to A12 to get the twelve months in the first column. Now enter some values into B1 and C1. Select those two cells, and double-click the fill handle. This fills automatically the data block B1:C12.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10713				0	ga	Using a Defined Series				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749				0	ga	Select the cell range in the sheet that you want to fill.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754				0	ga	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Edit - Fill - Series\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10716				0	ga	Select the parameters for the series. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10731				0	ga	If you select a \<emph\>linear\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is \<emph\>added\</emph\> to each consecutive number in the series to create the next value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN1073C				0	ga	If you select a \<emph\>growth\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is \<emph\>multiplied\</emph\> by each consecutive number to create the next value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10747				0	ga	If you select a \<emph\>date\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is added to the time unit that you specify.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3159173				0	ga	\<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060400.xhp\" name=\"Sort lists\"\>Sort lists\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
