helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153923				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>scaling; in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153923				0	ga	 \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091400.xhp\" name=\"Scaling\"\>Scaling\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3147173				0	ga	More detailed information about scaling in \<emph\>\<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Math\</emph\> as well as some examples can be found here. (The quotation marks in this text are for emphasis purposes only and are not part of the examples.)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3156316				0	ga	The factorial is not scaled (example: "fact stack{a#b}" and "fact {a over b}") but is oriented using the baseline or center of the arguments.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3150756				0	ga	Brackets always have a fixed size as well. This applies to all symbols that can be used as brackets. Compare "(((a)))", "( stack{a#b#c})", "(a over b)."				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3147570				0	ga	Brackets preceded by "left" or "right", however, are always adjusted to the argument. See "left(left(left(a right)right)right)", "left(stack{a#b#c}right)", "left(a over b right)."				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3145206				0	ga	Some \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091300.xhp\" name=\"Attributes\"\>Attributes\</link\> have fixed sizes; do not change these if they are placed above a long symbol.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3154694				0	ga	The spaces in the examples are required for the correct structure. You may not delete them when making entries in the Commands window.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
