helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149018				0	ga	\<variable id=\"keyboard\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/keyboard.xhp\" name=\"Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)\"\>Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150298				0	ga	You can control $[officename] Math without a mouse.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150343				0	ga	Inserting a Formula Directly				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659				0	ga	If you want to insert a formula into a text document, and you already know the correct writing, you can proceed as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153818				0	ga	Write the formula into your text				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153915				0	ga	Select the formula				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150213				0	ga	Choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154767				0	ga	Inserting a Formula using a Window				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3149875				0	ga	If you want to use the $[officename] Math interface to edit a formula, choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\> without any text selected.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391				0	ga	The cursor waits in the Commands window and you can type the formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ga	Working with Limits				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	bm_id8404492				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>limits;in sums/integrals\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>integral limits\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	hd_id1892147				0	ga	\<variable id=\"limits\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/limits.xhp\"\>Working with Limits\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	hd_id9881893				0	ga	How can I define the limits in a Sum or Integral formula?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id6504409				0	ga	You want to insert a summation formula like "summation of s^k from k = 0 to n" at the cursor in a Writer text document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8811304				0	ga	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Object - Formula\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9641712				0	ga	To enable lower and upper limits, click additionally the \<emph\>Upper and Lower Limits\</emph\> icon.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3304239				0	ga	In the input window, the first placeholder or marker is selected, and you can start to enter the lower limit: 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8679158				0	ga	k = 0				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8471327				0	ga	Press F4 to advance to the next marker, and enter the upper limit:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id4731894				0	ga	n				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id759300				0	ga	Press F4 to advance to the next marker, and enter the summand:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id651875				0	ga	s^k				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id6756187				0	ga	Now the formula is complete. Click into your text document outside the formula to leave the formula editor.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id5866267				0	ga	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Object - Formula\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id1965697				0	ga	Click in the input window and enter the following line:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9004486				0	ga	int from{a} to{b} f(x)`dx				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3877071				0	ga	If you don't like the font of the letters f and x, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Fonts\</item\> and select other fonts. Click the \<emph\>Default\</emph\> button to use the new fonts as default from now on.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3021332				0	ga	If you need the formula within a line of text, the limits increase the line height. You can choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Text Mode\</item\> to place the limits besides the Sum or Integral symbol, which reduces the line height.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id260322				0	ga	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03090909.xhp\"\>Example of Integral and Sum ranges\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
