helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ga	TimeSerial Function [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	bm_id3143271				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>TimeSerial function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143271				0	ga	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030205.xhp\" name=\"TimeSerial Function [Runtime]\"\>TimeSerial Function [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3156344				0	ga	Calculates a serial time value for the specified hour, minute, and second parameters that are passed as numeric value. You can then use this value to calculate the difference between times.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146794				0	ga	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3150792				0	ga	TimeSerial (hour, minute, second)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148797				0	ga	Return value:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3154908				0	ga	Date				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154124				0	ga	Parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193				0	ga	\<emph\>hour:\</emph\> Any integer expression that indicates the hour of the time that is used to determine the serial time value. Valid values: 0-23.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3159252				0	ga	\<emph\>minute:\</emph\> Any integer expression that indicates the minute of the time that is used to determine the serial time value. In general, use values between 0 and 59. However, you can also use values that lie outside of this range, where the number of minutes influence the hour value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3161831				0	ga	\<emph\>second:\</emph\> Any integer expression that indicates the second of the time that is used to determine the serial time value. In general, you can use values between 0 and 59. However, you can also use values that lie outside of this range, where the number seconds influences the minute value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3155854				0	ga	\<emph\>Examples:\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153952				0	ga	12, -5, 45 corresponds to 11, 55, 45				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3147349				0	ga	12, 61, 45 corresponds to 13, 2, 45				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3147426				0	ga	12, 20, -2 corresponds to 12, 19, 58				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153365				0	ga	12, 20, 63 corresponds to 12, 21, 4				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3146985				0	ga	You can use the TimeSerial function to convert any time into a single value that you can use to calculate time differences.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3155308				0	ga	The TimeSerial function returns the type Variant with VarType 7 (Date). This value is stored internally as a double-precision number between 0 and 0.9999999999. As opposed to the DateSerial or DateValue function, where the serial date values are calculated as days relative to a fixed date, you can calculate with values returned by the TimeSerial function, but you cannot evaluate them.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3149482				0	ga	In the TimeValue function, you can pass a string as a parameter containing the time. For the TimeSerial function, however, you can pass the individual parameters (hour, minute, second) as separate numeric expressions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154790				0	ga	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
