helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020305.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159413				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>Seek statement\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020305.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159413				0	ga	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03020305.xhp\" name=\"Seek Statement [Runtime]\"\>Seek Statement [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020305.xhp	0	help	par_id3153381				0	ga	Sets the position for the next writing or reading in a file that was opened with the Open statement.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020305.xhp	0	help	par_id2100589				0	ga	For random access files, the Seek statement sets the number of the next record to be accessed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020305.xhp	0	help	par_id5444807				0	ga	For all other files, the Seek statement sets the byte position at which the next operation is to occur.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020305.xhp	0	help	par_id3156280				0	ga	See also: \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03020103.xhp\" name=\"Open\"\>Open\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03020304.xhp\" name=\"Seek\"\>Seek\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020305.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145785				0	ga	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020305.xhp	0	help	par_id3145273				0	ga	Seek[#FileNumber], Position (As Long)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020305.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154321				0	ga	Parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020305.xhp	0	help	par_id3153952	9			0	ga	\<emph\>FileNumber: \</emph\>The data channel number used in the Open statement.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020305.xhp	0	help	par_id3145366	10			0	ga	\<emph\>Position: \</emph\>Position for the next writing or reading. Position can be a number between 1 and 2,147,483,647. According to the file type, the position indicates the number of the record (files in the Random mode) or the byte position (files in the Binary, Output, Append or Input mode). The first byte in a file is position 1, the second byte is position 2, and so on.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
