helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090203.xhp	0	help	par_id3145251				0	ga	end sub				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ga	Using Procedures and Functions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149456				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>procedures\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;using\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;passing to procedures and functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters;for procedures and functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters;passing by reference or value\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;scope\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>scope of variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>GLOBAL variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PUBLIC variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PRIVATE variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;return value type\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>return value type of functions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149456				0	ga	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020300.xhp\"\>Using Procedures and Functions\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767				0	ga	The following describes the basic use of procedures and functions in $[officename] Basic.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151215				0	ga	When you create a new module, $[officename] Basic automatically inserts a SUB called "Main". This default name has nothing to do with the order or the starting point of a $[officename] Basic project. You can also safely rename this SUB.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154124				0	ga	Procedures (SUBS) and functions (FUNCTIONS) help you maintaining a structured overview by separating a program into logical pieces.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193				0	ga	One benefit of procedures and functions is that, once you have developed a program code containing task components, you can use this code in another project.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153770				0	ga	Passing Variables to Procedures (SUB) and Functions (FUNCTION)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155414				0	ga	Variables can be passed to both procedures and functions. The SUB or FUNCTION must be declared to expect parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710				0	ga	SUB SubName(\<emph\>Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...\</emph\>)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151114				0	ga	Program code				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975				0	ga	END SUB				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577				0	ga	The SUB is called using the following syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3159154				0	ga	SubName(Value1, Value2,...)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147124				0	ga	The parameters passed to a SUB must fit to those specified in the SUB declaration.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147397				0	ga	The same process applies to FUNCTIONS. In addition, functions always return a function result. The result of a function is defined by assigning the return value to the function name:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149412				0	ga	FUNCTION FunctionName(Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...) As Type				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284				0	ga	Program code				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799				0	ga	\<emph\>FunctionName=Result\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150716				0	ga	End Function				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153839				0	ga	The FUNCTION is called using the following syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146914				0	ga	Variable=FunctionName(Parameter1, Parameter2,...)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_idN107B3				0	ga	You can also use the fully qualified name to call a procedure or function:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Library.Module.Macro()\</item\>\<br/\> For example, to call the Autotext macro from the Gimmicks library, use the following command:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Gimmicks.AutoText.Main()\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156276				0	ga	Passing Variables by Value or Reference				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155765				0	ga	Parameters can be passed to a SUB or a FUNCTION either by reference or by value. Unless otherwise specified, a parameter is always passed by reference. That means that a SUB or a FUNCTION gets the parameter and can read and modify its value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640				0	ga	If you want to pass a parameter by value insert the key word "ByVal" in front of the parameter when you call a SUB or FUNCTION, for example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150042				0	ga	Result = Function(\<emph\>ByVal\</emph\> Parameter)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149258				0	ga	In this case, the original content of the parameter will not be modified by the FUNCTION since it only gets the value and not the parameter itself.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150982				0	ga	Scope of Variables				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149814				0	ga	A variable defined within a SUB or FUNCTION, only remains valid until the procedure is exited. This is known as a "local" variable. In many cases, you need a variable to be valid in all procedures, in every module of all libraries, or after a SUB or FUNCTION is exited.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154186				0	ga	Declaring Variables Outside a SUB or FUNCTION				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150208				0	ga	GLOBAL VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145258				0	ga	The variable is valid as long as the $[officename] session lasts.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153198				0	ga	PUBLIC VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088				0	ga	The variable is valid in all modules.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3158212				0	ga	PRIVATE VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152994				0	ga	The variable is only valid in this module.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150886				0	ga	DIM VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150368				0	ga	The variable is only valid in this module.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id5097506				0	ga	Example for private variables				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id8738975				0	ga	Enforce private variables to be private across modules by setting CompatibilityMode(true).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
