helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ga	Cos Function [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154923				0	ga	\<bookmark_value\>Cos function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154923				0	ga	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03080102.xhp\" name=\"Cos Function [Runtime]\"\>Cos Function [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3159413				0	ga	Calculates the cosine of an angle. The angle is specified in radians. The result lies between -1 and 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3150358				0	ga	Using the angle Alpha, the Cos-Function calculates the ratio of the length of the side that is adjacent to the angle, divided by the length of the hypotenuse in a right-angled triangle.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3154141				0	ga	Cos(Alpha) = Adjacent/Hypotenuse				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154125				0	ga	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3145172				0	ga	Cos (Number)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156214				0	ga	Return value:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3150449				0	ga	Double				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153969				0	ga	Parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3153770				0	ga	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Numeric expression that specifies an angle in radians that you want to calculate the cosine for.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3145749				0	ga	To convert degrees to radians, multiply degrees by pi/180. To convert radians to degrees, multiply radians by 180/pi.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664				0	ga	degree=(radian*180)/pi				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3146985				0	ga	radian=(degree*pi)/180				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3152885				0	ga	Pi is here the fixed circle constant with the rounded value 3.14159...				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153951				0	ga	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3155855				0	ga	REM The following example allows for a right-angled triangle the input of				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3149484				0	ga	REM secant and angle (in degrees) and calculates the length of the hypotenuse:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3147428				0	ga	Sub ExampleCosinus				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3150010				0	ga	REM rounded Pi = 3.14159				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3149959				0	ga	Dim d1 as Double, dAngle as Double				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3144764				0	ga	d1 = InputBox$ (""Enter the length of the adjacent side: ","Adjacent")				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3154491				0	ga	dAngle = InputBox$ ("Enter the angle Alpha (in degrees): ","Alpha")				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3151074				0	ga	Print "The length of the hypothenuse is"; (d1 / cos (dAngle * Pi / 180))				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3149583				0	ga	End Sub				2002-02-02 02:02:02
